1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,825
Zoya has three groups of
buyers, and one small kitchen.

2
00:00:04,125 --> 00:00:06,939
Nani can't make laddoos
for everyone, every day.

3
00:00:07,489 --> 00:00:10,088
So which group should
Laddoo Lab serve first?

4
00:00:10,738 --> 00:00:13,078
In Nani's kitchen, every hour counts.

5
00:00:13,428 --> 00:00:18,123
Protein bars for gym-goers would need
new ingredients and new machines.

6
00:00:18,473 --> 00:00:22,661
A four o'clock office snack would
need deliveries across the city every

7
00:00:22,661 --> 00:00:23,561
day.

8
00:00:23,511 --> 00:00:26,710
And tiffin parents already
love Nani's recipe.

9
00:00:26,710 --> 00:00:28,572
The choice is not easy.

10
00:00:29,222 --> 00:00:31,569
This choice is called market targeting.

11
00:00:31,869 --> 00:00:36,596
Kotler and Armstrong define a target
market as a set of buyers who share

12
00:00:36,596 --> 00:00:41,124
common needs or characteristics,
that the company decides to serve.

13
00:00:41,474 --> 00:00:44,238
In simple words: you
can't serve everyone well.

14
00:00:44,238 --> 00:00:46,243
So pick who you will serve best.

15
00:00:46,893 --> 00:00:50,260
Let's watch Zoya score each
group on three questions.

16
00:00:50,610 --> 00:00:51,510
One.

17
00:00:51,048 --> 00:00:53,298
Is the group big enough, and growing?

18
00:00:53,648 --> 00:00:56,546
Tiffin parents and office
snackers score high.

19
00:00:56,546 --> 00:00:57,893
Gym-goers are fewer.

20
00:00:58,243 --> 00:00:59,143
Two.

21
00:00:58,782 --> 00:01:00,897
How tough is the competition?

22
00:01:01,247 --> 00:01:03,760
Gym shelves are full of protein bars.

23
00:01:03,760 --> 00:01:06,991
But tiffin sweets with no
refined sugar are rare.

24
00:01:07,341 --> 00:01:08,241
Three.

25
00:01:07,879 --> 00:01:11,116
Does it fit what we do best,
and what we can afford?

26
00:01:11,466 --> 00:01:14,838
Nani's healthy sweet fits
a tiffin box perfectly.

27
00:01:15,188 --> 00:01:16,178
Add it up.

28
00:01:16,178 --> 00:01:17,647
Tiffin parents win.

29
00:01:17,647 --> 00:01:20,159
Laddoo Lab's target is busy parents.

30
00:01:20,809 --> 00:01:22,919
You can't be everything to everyone.

31
00:01:22,919 --> 00:01:24,296
Choose who you serve.

32
00:01:24,996 --> 00:01:27,489
Companies target in four main ways.

33
00:01:27,839 --> 00:01:30,180
Undifferentiated, or mass marketing.

34
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:32,295
One offer for the whole market.

35
00:01:32,645 --> 00:01:34,253
Differentiated marketing.

36
00:01:34,253 --> 00:01:36,546
A separate offer for each segment.

37
00:01:36,896 --> 00:01:39,153
Concentrated, or niche marketing.

38
00:01:39,153 --> 00:01:41,320
One segment, served very well.

39
00:01:41,670 --> 00:01:42,965
And micromarketing.

40
00:01:42,965 --> 00:01:46,146
Offers shaped for one
area, or even one person.

41
00:01:46,796 --> 00:01:48,928
So which one is Laddoo Lab using?

42
00:01:49,278 --> 00:01:50,328
Concentrated.

43
00:01:50,328 --> 00:01:52,910
One small brand, one clear group.

44
00:01:52,910 --> 00:01:54,026
Busy parents.

45
00:01:54,376 --> 00:01:58,980
Later, with more kitchens and more
money, it could add gym-goers and office

46
00:01:58,980 --> 00:01:59,880
snackers.

47
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:02,154
That would be differentiated marketing.

48
00:02:02,804 --> 00:02:04,772
Where do we see this at work today?

49
00:02:05,122 --> 00:02:06,269
Mass marketing.

50
00:02:06,269 --> 00:02:09,969
A salt maker sells the same
pack to almost every home.

51
00:02:10,319 --> 00:02:11,377
Differentiated.

52
00:02:11,377 --> 00:02:15,980
A bank has separate accounts for
students, families and senior citizens.

53
00:02:16,330 --> 00:02:17,384
Concentrated.

54
00:02:17,384 --> 00:02:20,478
A small bakery makes
only gluten-free bread.

55
00:02:20,828 --> 00:02:21,977
Micromarketing.

56
00:02:21,977 --> 00:02:26,323
A food delivery app suggests
dishes from your own past orders.

57
00:02:26,973 --> 00:02:28,897
But does targeting have a cost?

58
00:02:29,247 --> 00:02:30,294
The good side.

59
00:02:30,294 --> 00:02:32,870
Money and effort go where they work best.

60
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:34,359
The hard side.

61
00:02:34,359 --> 00:02:36,739
Saying no means losing some buyers.

62
00:02:36,739 --> 00:02:39,230
And depending on one group is risky.

63
00:02:39,580 --> 00:02:40,615
Arjun worries.

64
00:02:40,615 --> 00:02:44,184
What happens in the summer
holidays, when schools close?

65
00:02:44,534 --> 00:02:46,683
Tiffin sales could drop for weeks.

66
00:02:47,033 --> 00:02:51,470
So Zoya keeps a small list of office
snackers, as a second group to grow

67
00:02:51,470 --> 00:02:52,370
later.

68
00:02:52,402 --> 00:02:55,458
And notice, she targets
parents, not children.

69
00:02:55,458 --> 00:02:57,926
Marketing to children needs extra care.

70
00:02:58,276 --> 00:02:59,176
The lesson?

71
00:02:59,102 --> 00:03:01,598
Choose a focus, but plan for the risks.

72
00:03:02,248 --> 00:03:03,193
Quick check.

73
00:03:03,193 --> 00:03:07,596
A shoe company sells running shoes,
school shoes and office shoes, each

74
00:03:07,596 --> 00:03:08,901
to its own group.

75
00:03:08,901 --> 00:03:11,044
Which targeting strategy is this?

76
00:03:12,944 --> 00:03:14,000
Differentiated!

77
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:16,302
A separate offer for each segment.

78
00:03:16,902 --> 00:03:17,915
So, remember.

79
00:03:18,215 --> 00:03:21,003
Targeting means choosing
which segments to serve.

80
00:03:21,353 --> 00:03:24,972
Score each one on size and
growth, competition, and fit.

81
00:03:25,322 --> 00:03:29,884
And pick a strategy: mass,
differentiated, concentrated or micro.

82
00:03:30,484 --> 00:03:33,153
Next time, we'll explore
market positioning.
